WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.349 --> 00:00:02.767
The company spent millions building its platform.

2
00:00:02.969 --> 00:00:05.333
But one missing rule exposed its customers.

3
00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:08.145
Imagine a company with thousands of customers,

4
00:00:08.265 --> 00:00:12.338
a polished application, and a powerful database holding sensitive business

5
00:00:12.458 --> 00:00:13.140
information.

6
00:00:14.088 --> 00:00:15.147
Users had to log in.

7
00:00:15.349 --> 00:00:18.189
But the backend failed to verify whether each user was

8
00:00:18.309 --> 00:00:21.506
actually allowed to access the specific record they requested.

9
00:00:23.088 --> 00:00:25.040
The mistake was broken access control.

10
00:00:25.235 --> 00:00:28.433
A request could reach another customer's data because ownership was

11
00:00:28.553 --> 00:00:29.919
never properly checked.

12
00:00:33.089 --> 00:00:35.369
Now imagine invoices, customer records,

13
00:00:35.489 --> 00:00:39.498
and confidential business information becoming accessible to someone who should

14
00:00:39.618 --> 00:00:40.794
never have seen them.

15
00:00:45.089 --> 00:00:46.700
The damage goes beyond data.

16
00:00:46.897 --> 00:00:50.326
Customer trust, legal exposure, business reputation,

17
00:00:50.446 --> 00:00:53.129
and operational continuity can all be at risk.

18
00:00:55.089 --> 00:00:58.764
The fix begins with security by design: enforce authorization on

19
00:00:58.884 --> 00:01:01.957
every sensitive request, deny access by default,

20
00:01:02.165 --> 00:01:06.176
use least-privilege database accounts, and test access rules before deployment.

21
00:01:12.576 --> 00:01:15.638
A beautiful application is not a secure application.

22
00:01:15.826 --> 00:01:17.725
Security must be designed into the system,

23
00:01:17.967 --> 00:01:19.540
not added after the damage.

24
00:01:19.829 --> 00:01:22.579
Would you trust your business data without access controls?

25
00:01:22.786 --> 00:01:24.781
Follow for more stories behind the technology.
